D={
"Breakup of Pangaea":"Pangaea, the supercontinent assembled around 335 million years ago, began to rift about 200 million years ago as magma welled up along cracks; the eruption of the Central Atlantic Magmatic Province coincided with the end-Triassic mass extinction. First the Atlantic opened between North America and Africa, then Gondwana split into South America, Africa, Antarctica, Australia and India. India drifted north at up to 15 cm a year and collided with Asia about 50 million years ago, raising the Himalaya. The separation of continents explains why marsupials dominate Australia and why fossils of the same reptiles appear in India, Africa and Antarctica.",
"Chicxulub asteroid impact":"About 66 million years ago an asteroid roughly 10 km wide struck the Yucatán Peninsula, gouging a crater 180 km across and releasing energy equal to billions of atomic bombs. Tsunamis, global wildfires and a 'impact winter' of soot and sulphate aerosols blocked sunlight for years, collapsing food chains. About three-quarters of all species died, including all non-bird dinosaurs; the iridium-rich clay layer found worldwide (identified by Luis and Walter Alvarez in 1980) is its fingerprint. India's Deccan Traps eruptions at the same time added climate stress. Small mammals survived and later diversified, eventually leading to primates and humans.",
"Onset of the Quaternary ice ages":"Around 2.6 million years ago, Earth's climate cooled enough for great ice sheets to grow and shrink over the northern continents in cycles paced by variations in Earth's orbit (Milankovitch cycles), at first every 41,000 years and, after about 1 million years ago, every 100,000 years. Africa became drier and more variable, with savannas expanding — a backdrop to the emergence of the genus Homo and stone tool making. Falling sea levels repeatedly exposed land bridges such as Beringia and Sundaland, which humans later crossed.",
"Toba super-eruption":"The Youngest Toba eruption in Sumatra about 74,000 years ago ejected roughly 2,800 cubic km of material, leaving Lake Toba's caldera and spreading ash over India, where a layer up to several metres thick still lies. Scientists once argued it caused a 'volcanic winter' and a human population bottleneck. Excavations at Jwalapuram in Andhra Pradesh found stone tools above and below the ash, and studies in South Africa suggest people coped, so its human impact is now debated rather than assumed catastrophic.",
"Last Glacial Maximum":"Between about 26,000 and 19,000 years ago ice sheets reached their greatest recent extent, covering Canada, Scandinavia and the British Isles under up to 3 km of ice. Sea level was about 120 m lower, joining Britain to Europe, Sri Lanka to India and Siberia to Alaska. People retreated to refugia such as southern France, Iberia and Ukraine, hunting mammoth and reindeer and building mammoth-bone huts. As the ice melted, they recolonised northern lands and the Americas were settled.",
"Younger Dryas":"About 12,900 years ago, while the world was warming, the Northern Hemisphere abruptly snapped back to near-glacial cold for about 1,200 years, probably because meltwater weakened the Atlantic's ocean currents. In the Levant the cold and drought may have pushed Natufian foragers to cultivate wild cereals, a step toward farming. It ended suddenly around 11,700 years ago, marking the start of the Holocene, the warm, stable climate in which agriculture and civilisation developed.",
"Quaternary megafauna extinction":"Between about 50,000 and 10,000 years ago, most large animals over 45 kg disappeared from Australia, the Americas and northern Eurasia: mammoths, mastodons, giant ground sloths, Diprotodon, Smilodon and many others. The timing often follows human arrival — Australia around 45,000 years ago, the Americas around 13,000 years ago — suggesting hunting played a big part, combined with climate change at the end of the Ice Age. Africa and South Asia, where animals evolved alongside humans, lost far fewer species.",
"End of the Green Sahara":"During the African Humid Period (about 14,500–5,500 years ago), stronger monsoon rains turned the Sahara into grassland with lakes, hippos and crocodiles; rock art shows cattle herders and swimmers. As Earth's orbit shifted, rains weakened and the Sahara dried, sometimes abruptly, around 5,500 years ago. Herders moved toward the Nile and the Sahel, a migration that some historians link to the concentration of people that led to Egyptian civilisation.",
"4.2-kiloyear drought":"Around 2200 BCE a severe, centuries-long drought affected regions from the Mediterranean to India and China. It coincides with the collapse of the Akkadian Empire, the end of Egypt's Old Kingdom, and changes in the Indus Valley, where cities later declined as monsoons weakened. It is used to define the boundary between the Middle and Late Holocene (the Meghalayan Age, named after a cave in Meghalaya, India).",
"Thera (Santorini) eruption":"Around 1600 BCE the volcanic island of Thera exploded in one of the largest eruptions of the past 10,000 years. It buried the Minoan town of Akrotiri under ash (its inhabitants had already fled), triggered tsunamis on Crete, and probably weakened Minoan civilisation. Some scholars think it inspired the legend of Atlantis.",
"Eruption of Vesuvius":"On 24–25 August 79 CE (some evidence suggests October), Vesuvius buried Pompeii and Herculaneum under ash and pyroclastic flows, killing perhaps 2,000 people. Pliny the Younger described the eruption in letters; his uncle Pliny the Elder died trying to rescue people. Preserved houses, bread and graffiti make Pompeii a snapshot of everyday Roman life.",
"Volcanic winter of 536":"In 536 CE an eruption, probably in Iceland, filled the Northern Hemisphere sky with dust; Procopius wrote that the sun shone without brightness for a year. Summer temperatures fell sharply, crops failed from Ireland to China, and further eruptions in 539–540 deepened the cold of the Late Antique Little Ice Age. It was followed by the Plague of Justinian.",
"Tambora eruption":"Mount Tambora on Sumbawa, Indonesia, erupted in April 1815, the largest eruption in recorded history, killing over 70,000 people locally. Its aerosols caused 1816's 'Year Without a Summer', with crop failures in Europe and North America, and contributed to a cholera outbreak in Bengal. That cold summer, Mary Shelley wrote Frankenstein.",
"Krakatoa eruption":"In August 1883 Krakatoa in the Sunda Strait exploded with a sound heard 4,800 km away; tsunamis killed about 36,000 people. Ash produced vivid red sunsets worldwide; telegraphs spread the news within hours, making it the first global media disaster.",
"Plague of Athens":"In 430 BCE, in the second year of the Peloponnesian War, an epidemic struck Athens, crowded with refugees inside the Long Walls. It killed perhaps a quarter of the population, including the leader Pericles. Thucydides, who survived it, described its symptoms; its cause is still debated (typhoid is one candidate).",
"Antonine Plague":"Brought back by Roman troops from Mesopotamia around 165 CE, probably smallpox, it killed perhaps 5–10 million people across the empire, including possibly Emperor Lucius Verus. Galen described it. It weakened Rome's army and economy.",
"Plague of Justinian":"From 541 CE the bubonic plague (Yersinia pestis, confirmed by ancient DNA) spread from Egypt across the Byzantine Empire; Constantinople may have lost thousands a day at its peak. Recurrences for two centuries weakened Byzantium and Persia just before the Arab conquests.",
"Black Death":"Yersinia pestis, carried by fleas on rats and spread along trade routes from Central Asia, reached the Crimea in 1346 and Europe in 1347. Within five years it killed perhaps a third to half of Europe's population and many in the Middle East. Labour shortages raised wages, weakened serfdom and fuelled revolts, while persecutions of Jews followed. Repeated outbreaks continued until the 18th century.",
"Smallpox in the Americas":"Europeans brought smallpox, measles and influenza, to which Native Americans had no immunity. Smallpox reached Mexico in 1520 and devastated Tenochtitlan during Cortés's siege, and swept through the Inca Empire, killing the emperor Huayna Capac and triggering civil war. Epidemics may have killed up to 90% of the indigenous population over a century.",
"Cholera pandemics":"Cholera spread from the Ganges delta in 1817 along trade and military routes; seven pandemics followed. John Snow's 1854 study of a London water pump showed it spread by contaminated water, leading to modern sanitation.",
"Third plague pandemic":"Starting in Yunnan in the 1850s, plague reached Hong Kong (1894) and Bombay (1896), killing about 12 million in India. Alexandre Yersin identified the bacterium in 1894. British plague measures in India provoked protests.",
"Spanish flu":"The 1918–1920 H1N1 influenza pandemic infected about a third of the world and killed perhaps 50 million, including 12–17 million in India. Spread by troop movements at the end of World War I, it struck young adults especially hard.",
"HIV/AIDS pandemic":"HIV crossed from chimpanzees to humans in Central Africa in the early 20th century and was identified in 1983. About 40 million have died; antiretroviral drugs from 1996 turned it into a manageable condition.",
"COVID-19 pandemic":"SARS-CoV-2 emerged in Wuhan in late 2019 and was declared a pandemic in March 2020. Lockdowns, rapid mRNA vaccines and about 7 million recorded deaths (excess deaths perhaps much higher) made it the worst pandemic in a century.",
"Invention of writing":"Writing was invented independently at least four times: in Mesopotamia (cuneiform, about 3200 BCE), Egypt (hieroglyphs, about 3200 BCE), China (oracle bones, about 1250 BCE) and Mesoamerica (about 900–600 BCE). In Sumer it grew from clay tokens used for accounting to record goods, then laws, letters and literature. Writing let states keep records, collect taxes and preserve knowledge across generations — the dividing line between prehistory and history.",
"Unification of Egypt":"Around 3100 BCE Upper (southern) and Lower (northern) Egypt were united under one king, traditionally Menes, usually identified with Narmer. The Narmer Palette shows him wearing both crowns and smiting enemies. Unification created the first territorial state, with Memphis as capital.",
"Battle of Kadesh":"Around 1274 BCE Ramesses II fought the Hittite king Muwatalli II at Kadesh in Syria, in perhaps the largest chariot battle ever (about 5,000 chariots). Ramesses was nearly trapped but rallied; both sides claimed victory. About 15 years later they signed the earliest known peace treaty.",
"Founding of Rome":"According to legend, Romulus founded Rome on 21 April 753 BCE after killing his twin Remus. Archaeology shows villages on the Palatine hill from about 1000 BCE that merged into a city by the 7th century BCE, ruled by kings, some Etruscan, until about 509 BCE.",
"Buddha's enlightenment":"Siddhartha Gautama, after years of asceticism, meditated under a pipal tree at Bodh Gaya and attained enlightenment (around the 5th century BCE). He gave his first sermon at Sarnath on the Four Noble Truths, founding the sangha.",
"Persian Wars":"The Ionian Greek cities revolted against Persia (499 BCE) with Athenian help. Darius's army lost at Marathon (490 BCE); Xerxes' huge invasion was delayed at Thermopylae by Leonidas's Spartans and defeated at sea at Salamis (480) and on land at Plataea (479). Victory gave Athens confidence and an empire, leading to its golden age.",
"Alexander's invasion of India":"In 327–325 BCE Alexander crossed the Hindu Kush, took Taxila's surrender and defeated King Porus at the Hydaspes (Jhelum) in 326 BCE despite war elephants. His troops mutinied at the Beas river, and he returned via the Indus and the Gedrosian desert. The invasion weakened northwestern kingdoms, helping Chandragupta Maurya build his empire.",
"Kalinga War":"About 261 BCE Ashoka conquered Kalinga (Odisha). His Rock Edict XIII says 150,000 were deported, 100,000 killed and many more died. Remorse turned him to Buddhism and dhamma, renouncing conquest by war.",
"Unification of China under Qin":"Between 230 and 221 BCE, King Ying Zheng of Qin conquered Han, Zhao, Wei, Chu, Yan and Qi, ending the Warring States period. He declared himself First Emperor and imposed uniform laws, writing, currency and measures.",
"Punic Wars":"Rome and Carthage fought three wars (264–146 BCE). Rome won Sicily in the first, survived Hannibal's invasion of Italy in the second (winning at Zama in 202 BCE), and destroyed Carthage in the third, becoming master of the western Mediterranean.",
"Roman conquest of Gaul":"Julius Caesar campaigned in Gaul from 58 to 50 BCE, defeating Vercingetorix at Alesia (52 BCE). About a million Gauls may have died. The victory gave Caesar wealth and loyal troops he used to seize power in Rome.",
"Roman conquest of Britain":"Claudius invaded Britain in 43 CE. Boudica's revolt (60–61 CE) burned London but failed; Hadrian's Wall (122 CE) marked the northern frontier. Rome left Britain about 410.",
"Assassination of Julius Caesar":"On 15 March 44 BCE, about 60 senators led by Brutus and Cassius stabbed Caesar in the Theatre of Pompey, fearing he would make himself king. Instead of restoring the Republic, it led to civil war and the rise of Augustus.",
"Crucifixion of Jesus":"Around 30–33 CE, Jesus of Nazareth was crucified in Jerusalem by order of the Roman governor Pontius Pilate. His followers proclaimed his resurrection, founding Christianity, which spread across the Roman Empire.",
"Fall of the Western Roman Empire":"Pressure from Goths, Vandals and Huns, civil wars, economic decline and dependence on barbarian soldiers weakened the West. Visigoths sacked Rome in 410 and Vandals in 455. In 476 the Germanic general Odoacer deposed Emperor Romulus Augustulus. The eastern empire continued as Byzantium.",
"Hijra and rise of Islam":"In 622 Muhammad and his followers migrated from Mecca to Medina, the start of the Islamic calendar. By 630 he had returned to Mecca, and by his death in 632 most of Arabia was united under Islam.",
"Arab conquest of Sindh":"In 712 Muhammad bin Qasim, a 17-year-old Umayyad general, defeated King Dahir and conquered Sindh, bringing Islam to the Indus valley.",
"Battle of Tours":"In 732 Charles Martel's Franks defeated the Umayyad army of Abd al-Rahman near Tours, halting Muslim expansion north of the Pyrenees.",
"Battle of Talas":"In 751 an Abbasid and Tibetan-allied army defeated a Tang Chinese army near the Talas river, ending Tang expansion into Central Asia; Chinese prisoners are said to have spread papermaking.",
"Chola naval expedition to Srivijaya":"In 1025 Rajendra Chola I sent a fleet across the Bay of Bengal to raid Srivijaya's ports, including Kadaram (Kedah), to protect Chola trade with China.",
"Battle of Hastings":"On 14 October 1066 William of Normandy defeated Harold Godwinson, beginning the Norman Conquest of England.",
"First Crusade":"Pope Urban II's call (1095) led armies to capture Jerusalem in 1099, with a massacre of its inhabitants.",
"Second Battle of Tarain":"In 1192 Muhammad of Ghor defeated Prithviraj Chauhan, opening north India to the Delhi Sultanate.",
"Mongol conquests":"From 1206 to 1300 the Mongols conquered China, Central Asia, Persia and Russia, creating the largest land empire.",
"Destruction of Nalanda":"Around 1193 Bakhtiyar Khalji's forces sacked Nalanda university; its library is said to have burned for months.",
"Magna Carta":"In 1215 rebel barons forced King John to agree to a charter limiting royal power.",
"Sack of Baghdad":"In 1258 Hulagu's Mongols sacked Baghdad and killed the last Abbasid caliph, ending the Islamic Golden Age.",
"Fall of Constantinople":"On 29 May 1453 Mehmed II's Ottoman army, using giant cannon, breached the walls, ending the Byzantine Empire.",
}
